## Area 1 — Architecture & Dependency Model

### What the repository actually implements

DGLab is a PHP 8.4 monorepo/framework built around a **six-ring dependency architecture**:

```text
Outer Rim       UI / public entrypoints
     ↓
Outer Spokes    external adapters / bridges
     ↓
Inner Rim       HTTP/API pipeline
     ↓
Inner Spokes    application services
     ↓
Hub             aggregates / orchestration
     ↓
Core            framework primitives
```

The repository explicitly requires dependencies to point inward. The implementation largely follows this model. ([GitHub](https://github.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab))
The important distinction is that the **architecture is currently more mature than the application layer**. Core infrastructure has substantial implementation, while several Hub/Bridge/Spoke components remain depth-1/2 or stub-level. The README itself contains contradictory status statements, so repository code/tests should be treated as the source of truth. ([GitHub](https://github.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab))
### Actual request path

The current implementation is approximately:

```text
Caddy
  │
  ▼
Tengine
  │
  ▼
FrankenPHP worker
  │
  ▼
public/index.php
  │
  ├── Kernel boot ────────────────┐
  │                              │
  │     Container                │
  │     EventDispatcher          │
  │     Logger                   │
  │     ErrorHandler             │
  │     Router                   │
  │                              │
  ▼                              │
Kernel.handle(request)            │
  │                              │
  ▼                              │
RequestReceivedEvent              │
  │                              │
  ▼                              │
MiddlewarePipeline                │
  │                              │
  ▼                              │
Vanguard / outer adapter          │
  │                              │
  ▼                              │
FinalRequestHandler               │
  │                              │
  ▼                              │
Router                            │
  │                              │
  ▼                              │
Controller                        │
  │                              │
  ▼                              │
ResponseReadyEvent ◄─────────────┘
  │
  ▼
HTTP response
```

This isn't merely conceptual: `Kernel`, `MiddlewarePipeline`, `PerRequestHandler`, `Router`, `HttpBootstrapper`, and `public/index.php` implement these stages. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab/main/packages/core/kernel/src/Kernel.php))
### Particularly good architectural decisions

**1. Worker/request state separation**

This is probably the most important design decision in the current code.

The Kernel is deliberately **worker-scoped**, while the middleware cursor and DI `pulse()` instances are request/Fiber-scoped. The container uses `WeakMap<Fiber, ...>` so request state can disappear with the Fiber rather than accumulating inside a long-running FrankenPHP worker. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab/main/packages/core/kernel/src/Kernel.php))
That is exactly the sort of issue that normally breaks PHP applications when moving from PHP-FPM to persistent workers.

**2. Frozen infrastructure**

Router registration freezes after the first match, and middleware registration freezes after the first request. This prevents runtime mutation of the request graph. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab/main/packages/core/router/src/Router.php))
**3. Per-request middleware cursor**

The previous shared-cursor design was replaced with `PerRequestHandler`, giving every request its own cursor. This is important for Fiber concurrency and re-entrant middleware. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab/main/packages/core/middleware/src/PerRequestHandler.php))
**4. Explicit Kernel lifecycle**

```text
Unbooted
   │
   ▼
Booting ──failure──► Terminated
   │
   ▼
Booted
   │
   ▼
Handling
   │
   ▼
Booted
   │
   ▼
Terminating
   │
   ▼
Terminated
```

The Kernel also imposes explicit boot/request/termination ceilings and records lifecycle events. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab/main/packages/core/kernel/src/Kernel.php))
---

### Architectural issues I would carry into the final SPEC

#### A. `public/index.php` is currently doing too much

The public entrypoint directly constructs:

- Container
- Event dispatcher
- Logger
- Error handler
- Vanguard
- Contract registry
- WAF
- Kernel
- bootstrappers
- routes
- controller bindings

That means the **Outer Rim is currently also a composition root**.

It works for a walking skeleton, but I would not preserve this structure for the production architecture.

The eventual composition should be closer to:

```text
public/index.php
       │
       ▼
ApplicationFactory
       │
       ▼
Kernel
       │
       ├── Core services
       ├── Hub services
       ├── Bridges
       ├── Spokes
       └── application routes
```

This becomes particularly important once database, cache, encryption, authentication, authorization and multiple adapters arrive.

#### B. The architecture has a potentially dangerous distinction between **worker-scoped singleton** and **request-scoped service**

The container explicitly supports both:

```text
singleton → worker lifetime
pulse      → Fiber/request lifetime
transient  → construction lifetime
```

That is powerful, but it creates a major architectural invariant:

> **A worker-scoped service must never retain request-specific state.**

This needs to become a formal architecture rule and preferably a testable architectural fitness function. The current code has the mechanisms, but the application architecture still needs to enforce their correct usage. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab/main/packages/core/container/src/Container.php))
#### C. The "six rings" aren't yet fully represented in code

The Core is concrete and substantial.

The upper layers are still emerging. For example, the current public bootstrap explicitly describes JWT, rate limiting and audit functionality as pass-through stubs. ([GitHub](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab/main/public/index.php))
So I would describe the current architecture as:

> **A well-developed Core runtime with a partially realized concentric application architecture.**

Rather than calling the whole six-ring architecture production-complete.

#### D. There is already evidence of documentation/state drift

The README says the walking skeleton and full Pulse trace have been completed, but its Milestone 0 table simultaneously lists `HUB-01`, `BRIDGE-01`, `ISPOKE-09`, and `ESPOKE-01` as not started. It also contains historical text saying `public/index.php` was a 503 placeholder despite later showing an implemented entrypoint. ([GitHub](https://github.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab))
For a contractor-facing architecture, I would introduce a **single machine-verifiable architecture status source** rather than allowing README, blueprints, ADRs, worklogs and implementation status to diverge.

---

