The repository currently has **8 implemented Core packages** covering DI, events, HTTP messages, middleware, router, configuration, logging and error handling. It also has blueprints for 31 Hub and 45 Spoke components, but those blueprints substantially exceed what is currently implemented. ([GitHub](https://github.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab))
That distinction matters.

### Current maturity

| Layer | Current state | Architectural role |
|---|---|---|
| Core | Strong implementation | Runtime primitives |
| Hub | Early/scaffolded | Business/domain composition |
| Inner Spokes | Mostly blueprint/specification | Application services |
| Bridges | Early/scaffolded | Infrastructure translation |
| Outer Spokes | Mostly blueprint/specification | External integrations |
| UI | Application-level | Presentation |
| Deploy | Substantial | Runtime infrastructure |

So the Wheel is currently **asymmetric**:

```text
                 UI       ░░
              Adapters   ░░░
                 API     ██
              Services  ░░░
                Hub     ░░
                Core    ██████████
```

That isn't inherently bad. In fact, for a framework being built from the bottom upward, it is expected.

But it means we should **not yet infer that the proposed domain architecture has been validated simply because the Core architecture works**.

---

# 3.2 The Hub is the critical boundary

The README describes the Hub as:

> Aggregates

That is a potentially good choice, but it needs to be made much more precise.

I recommend defining the Hub as:

```text
Hub =
    Domain entities
  + Value objects
  + Aggregates
  + Domain services
  + Domain events
  + Repository interfaces
```

and **not**:

```text
Hub =
    database models
  + controllers
  + HTTP clients
  + framework services
```

The latter would turn the Hub into a "god layer."

The dependency should therefore be:

```text
                 Inner Spoke
                     │
                     ▼
              Application Service
                     │
                     ▼
                  Aggregate
                     │
             ┌───────┴────────┐
             ▼                ▼
        Domain Event     Repository
                              │
                              ▼
                       Infrastructure
```

The repository interface belongs inward.

The implementation belongs outward.

---

# 3.3 Data architecture

This is the biggest area where the current repository needs further definition.

The current Core contains infrastructure primitives but does **not yet establish a production persistence architecture**. The README explicitly identifies **CORE-19 DBAL, CORE-15 Cache, CORE-14 Filesystem and CORE-16 Encryption** as the next persistence-oriented Core packages. ([GitHub](https://github.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab))
Therefore I would **not invent a database schema for DGLab yet**.

Instead, the architecture should establish ownership first.

### Recommended data ownership

```text
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │     Application    │
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              │
                              ▼
                    Application Services
                              │
                              ▼
                         Aggregates
                              │
                              ▼
                      Repository Ports
                              │
                ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐
                │                           │
                ▼                           ▼
          SQL Repository              Cache Repository
                │                           │
                ▼                           ▼
           PostgreSQL                   Redis/etc.
```

The important rule is:

> **No application/domain component should depend directly on PDO, Redis, filesystem APIs or vendor SDKs.**

Those should be infrastructure implementations of inward-facing contracts.

---

# 3.4 Database boundary

When CORE-19 arrives, I would structure the persistence subsystem approximately as:

```text
packages/core/dbal/
    src/
        Connection/
        Transaction/
        Query/
        Migration/
        Exception/
```

Then application-specific persistence should **not** live inside Core.

For example:

```text
app/
  Domain/
    User/
      User.php
      UserRepository.php

  Infrastructure/
    Persistence/
      PostgreSQL/
        PostgreSQLUserRepository.php
```

Dependency:

```text
UserRepository
      ▲
      │ implements
      │
PostgreSQLUserRepository
```

rather than:

```text
User
 │
 └── PostgreSQLConnection ❌
```

This keeps Core generic and the domain portable.

