## Area 7 — Application Composition & Runtime Boundary

The dependency direction is now clear enough that the next question is **where the assembled application actually belongs**.

### Current situation

The repository's runtime composition is concentrated in `public/index.php`. The file constructs and wires the container, configuration, logging, error handling, event dispatcher, Vanguard, kernel, bootstrappers, routes, middleware and controller bindings.

That is a valid **composition root**, but it currently combines several responsibilities:

```text
public/index.php
 ├── infrastructure construction
 ├── dependency registration
 ├── application registration
 ├── route registration
 ├── middleware registration
 ├── controller registration
 └── kernel startup
```

The important distinction is:

> **The composition root may know about every ring. Individual rings should not know about the composition root.**

So I would not move composition into Core. That would invert the architecture.

### Proposed target

Introduce an application-level composition boundary:

```text
public/index.php
        │
        ▼
ApplicationFactory
        │
        ├── createContainer()
        ├── registerCore()
        ├── registerHub()
        ├── registerSpokes()
        ├── registerBridges()
        ├── registerInfrastructure()
        ├── registerRoutes()
        ├── registerMiddleware()
        └── createKernel()
                 │
                 ▼
              Kernel
                 │
                 ▼
        request execution
```

`public/index.php` then becomes approximately:

```php
<?php

require dirname(__DIR__) . '/vendor/autoload.php';

$app = ApplicationFactory::create();

$app->run();
```

The exact API can differ; the architectural constraint matters more than the naming.

### Why this matters now

This is **not** primarily a code-cleanliness refactor.

It creates a hard boundary between:

**Composition knowledge**

```text
"Which implementation satisfies UserRepository?"
"Which middleware is enabled?"
"Which routes exist?"
"Which infrastructure adapter is active?"
```

and:

**Runtime/domain knowledge**

```text
"I need a UserRepository."
"I need to dispatch an event."
"I need the current request."
```

That separation becomes particularly valuable once Hub and the external spokes grow.

### Important constraint

`ApplicationFactory` should **not become a new god object**.

I would eventually split registration into explicit providers/modules:

```text
ApplicationFactory
   │
   ├── CoreProvider
   ├── DomainProvider
   ├── InfrastructureProvider
   ├── IntegrationProvider
   └── HttpProvider
```

But I would **not introduce that abstraction prematurely**. First extract the existing composition faithfully; then split providers when there is enough application complexity to justify them.

---

## Proposed architectural rule

**REQ-APP-001 — Composition Boundary**

> Application composition MUST occur outside Core and Hub. The composition root MAY depend on all enabled application rings and infrastructure implementations, while runtime and domain packages MUST NOT depend on the composition root.

**REQ-APP-002 — Thin Entry Point**

> `public/index.php` SHOULD contain only process/bootstrap concerns and MUST NOT contain business registration logic.

**REQ-APP-003 — No Service Locator**

> Application/domain code MUST receive dependencies through explicit constructor or method contracts and MUST NOT retrieve arbitrary services from the global container.

---

### Area 7 conclusion

The recommended direction is therefore:

**Keep the Wheel. Keep Core independent. Keep the root composition omniscient. Move that omniscience out of `public/index.php` into an explicit application composition layer.**

This gives us the missing boundary between **architecture** and **assembly** before Hub becomes substantially implemented.

**Next area:** I recommend we examine the **Hub/domain boundary and persistence model**, because that is where the architecture needs to become concrete enough for contractors to implement business capabilities.

