 ┌──────┼───────────────┐
 │      │               │
Kernel DBAL          Filesystem
 │                      │
Container             Crypto
```

That is a meaningful architectural improvement.

---

# 1. The biggest correction: products now start at the Spoke

This is probably the most important architectural change.

The earlier plan had Showcase/LMS sitting under `packages/hub/...`.

The implementation now puts Showcase here:

```text
packages/spoke/internal/showcase/
```

while Identity remains:

```text
packages/hub/identity/
```

That distinction is much cleaner.

### Hub is becoming platform capability

Identity is something multiple products consume.

Showcase is a product.

Therefore:

```text
Hub
├── Identity
├── Config
├── Audit
└── other shared platform capabilities

Spoke
├── Showcase
├── LMS
└── future products
```

This matches the new SDLC fusion document's explicit position that **Spokes are application entry points, not Hub**.

That resolves an important conceptual ambiguity from the previous architecture.

It also gives you a much stronger dependency rule:

```text
Showcase → Identity → Core
Showcase → DBAL → Core
```

rather than:

```text
Showcase → some Hub domain that happens to contain the product
```

I would preserve this distinction going forward.

---

# 2. HUB-04 Identity is the first serious platform capability

The Identity package is now considerably more than a placeholder.

It contains:

```text
Application
├── IdentityInterface
├── AuthenticatedUser
└── IdentityApplicationService

Domain
├── User
├── UserId
├── Email
├── RoleIdentifier
└── repository contract

Infrastructure
└── MySQLUserRepository

HTTP
└── AuthMiddleware

JWT
├── JwtSigner
└── JwtVerifier
```

This is a good bounded structure.

More importantly, it follows the intended dependency direction:

```text
HTTP
  ↓
Application
  ↓
Domain
  ↑
Infrastructure
```

Conceptually:

```text
AuthMiddleware
      ↓
IdentityInterface
      ↓
User aggregate
      ↑
MySQLUserRepository
```

That is much closer to an actual modular application than the earlier "platform package collection" architecture.

---

# 3. The Identity → RequestContext integration is not actually complete

There is an important discrepancy here.

`RequestContext` explicitly says:

> AuthMiddleware verifies credentials and stamps the RequestContext with `userId`.

But the actual `AuthMiddleware` does this:

```php
$request = $request->withAttribute('userId', $userId);
return $handler->handle($request);
```

It does **not** call:

```php
$requestContext->withUserId(...)
```

and it does not appear to retrieve or replace the Fiber-local `RequestContext`.

So the architecture currently has two possible sources of user identity:

```text
Request attribute:
    request->getAttribute('userId')

RequestContext:
    RequestContext::$userId
```

That is precisely the kind of split state ownership that your SPEC was trying to eliminate.

### The intended model should be one of these

Preferably:

```text
Authorization
      ↓
AuthMiddleware
      ↓
RequestContext::withUserId()
      ↓
Container::pulse(RequestContext)
```

Then application services can consistently obtain request context from the runtime.

The request attribute could still exist as an HTTP concern if deliberately documented, but it should not become a second authoritative identity channel.

This is therefore one of the remaining architectural gaps.

---

# 4. The JWT implementation has a much more serious interoperability problem

`JwtSigner` declares:

```php
private const HEADER = ['alg' => 'ES256', 'typ' => 'JWT'];
```

and uses:

```php
openssl_sign(..., OPENSSL_ALG_SHA256)
```

The resulting OpenSSL ECDSA signature is then base64url encoded directly.

However, standard JWS ES256 requires the signature to be a **64-octet `R || S` value**, with each component exactly 32 octets. ([RFC Editor](https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc7518/?utm_source=chatgpt.com "RFC 7518: JSON Web Algorithms (JWA) | RFC Editor"))

OpenSSL's ECDSA API commonly produces the ASN.1/DER representation instead.

So the current implementation appears to be:

```text
JWT ES256
   ↓
openssl_sign()
   ↓
DER ECDSA signature
   ↓
base64url
```

when JWS expects:

```text
JWT ES256
   ↓
ECDSA(R,S)
   ↓
32-byte R || 32-byte S
   ↓
base64url
```

That means the implementation can be internally self-consistent while still being **non-standard JWT/JWS interoperability**.

Your existing `JwtSignerVerifierTest` only proving signer → verifier round-tripping does not establish compatibility with standard JWT implementations.

This should be fixed before Identity is treated as an external authentication boundary.

There is also another issue in `JwtVerifier`:

```php
$payloadData = json_decode(...)
```

without validating the JWT header's `alg` against the expected algorithm.

The verifier should explicitly parse and enforce:

```text
alg == ES256
```

rather than treating the header as decorative metadata.

---

# 5. Identity persistence currently lacks a transaction boundary

This is another architectural issue worth addressing now rather than later.

`MySQLUserRepository::save()` does approximately:

```text
update/insert user
↓
delete all user_roles
↓
insert roles
```

There is no transaction around the operation.

So this can happen:

```text
users UPDATE        ✓
user_roles DELETE   ✓
role INSERT         ✗
```

and you now have a partially persisted aggregate.

This is particularly significant because the architecture plan says application services own transaction boundaries.

But:

```php
IdentityApplicationService
```

currently knows only:

```php
UserRepositoryInterface
PasswordHasher
```

There is no unit-of-work / transaction abstraction at the application boundary.

The same problem appears in Showcase.

For depth 2 this is understandable. For depth 3, it should be explicitly resolved.

---

# 6. Showcase is the real architectural milestone

`packages/spoke/internal/showcase/` is arguably the most important new code.

Its structure is now:

```text
Showcase
├── Application
│   ├── Command
│   ├── ProductApplicationInterface
│   └── ProductApplicationService
│
├── Domain
│   ├── Entity
│   ├── ValueObject
│   ├── Repository
│   └── Exception
│
├── Infrastructure
│   └── Persistence
│
└── migrations
```

That is a recognizably bounded product domain.

And the dependency direction is good:

```text
Showcase
 ├── IdentityInterface
 ├── Core DBAL
 └── its own Domain
```

instead of:

```text
Showcase → concrete MySQL infrastructure
Showcase → HTTP
Showcase → global application container
```

The export allow-list also now explicitly exposes only ten Showcase symbols:

```text
ProductApplicationInterface
CreateProductCommand
ProductId
Sku
Price
ProductTitle
ProductSlug
ProductNotFoundException
DuplicateSkuException
InvalidProductDataException
```

That is a particularly good design decision.

It makes the package API intentional rather than:

> everything under `src/` is effectively public.

---

# 7. But Showcase has two concrete domain bugs

### Authorization is incomplete

`createProduct()` performs authorization:

```php
$this->requireAnyRole(...)
```

but:

```php
publishProduct(ProductId $id)
```

does not.

So the intended authorization model is currently:

```text
create → authorized
publish → no authorization check
```

That is inconsistent.

The service should establish authorization for each protected application operation, not just creation.

### Published entities are not hydrated correctly

The repository constructs a `Product` with:

```php
createdAt: new DateTimeImmutable($row['created_at'])
```

but does not restore:

```text
updated_at
```

from the database.

Worse, if the persisted status is published:

```php
$product->publish();
```

is called during hydration.

That changes:

```php
updatedAt
```

to the current time.

So reading an old published product can effectively become:

```text
database:
    updated_at = 2026-09-20

hydrate:
    publish()
       ↓
    updated_at = 2026-09-26
```

without the database being changed.

That's a persistence/domain-state bug.

Hydration should restore persisted state rather than invoke a behavioral transition that means "publish now."

---

# 8. CORE-14 Filesystem is a useful Core addition, but the security claim is currently too strong

The new Filesystem abstraction is conceptually excellent.

It centralizes:

```text
path safety
atomic writes
stream limits
integrity checking
quarantine
metadata
```

behind:

```php
FilesystemInterface
```

which is exactly what a Core capability plug should do.

The internal decomposition is also good:

```text
Filesystem
 ├── PathGuard
 ├── AtomicWriter
 └── Quarantine
```

That is much better than putting all the mechanics in one 300-line class.

However, `PathGuard` currently has a significant containment flaw.

It checks:

```php
str_starts_with($resolved, $this->rootPath)
```

Suppose the configured root is:

```text
/tmp/data
```

and the attacker reaches:

```text
/tmp/database/file
```

String comparison says:

```text
/tmp/database
starts with
/tmp/data
```

which is true.

Filesystem containment must be boundary-aware:

```text
/tmp/data/
```

not merely:

```text
/tmp/data
```

So the check should effectively be:

```php
$root = rtrim($root, DIRECTORY_SEPARATOR) . DIRECTORY_SEPARATOR;

if (
    $resolved !== rtrim($root, DIRECTORY_SEPARATOR)
    && !str_starts_with($resolved, $root)
) {
    reject();
}
```

or equivalent canonical containment logic.

For something explicitly described as:

> path-traversal-proof

that needs to be corrected.

---

# 9. `writeStream()` does not currently meet the same integrity guarantees as `write()`

`write()` does:

```text
atomic write
→ read back
→ compare
→ quarantine if mismatch
```

But `writeStream()` does:

```text
atomic write
→ rename
→ metadata
```

There is no equivalent read-back integrity check.

There are also failure-handling weaknesses in `AtomicWriter::writeStream()`:

```php
$chunk = fread(...);

if ($chunk === false) {
    break;
}
```

A read failure therefore becomes:

```text
read error
→ break
→ rename partial temp file
→ report success
```

That's dangerous.

Likewise:

```php
fwrite($out, $chunk);
```

doesn't verify that the complete chunk was written.

So the filesystem component is architecturally promising but not yet at the "nuclear-grade" level that its documentation implies.

---

# 10. M4 health architecture is conceptually correct but only partially wired

The new health model is:

```text
/health/live
/health/ready
/health/dependencies
/health
```

That separation is absolutely the right operational model.

The distinction is clean:

```text
live
 └── process existence

ready
 └── can accept application traffic

dependencies
 └── detailed dependency health
```

And the liveness endpoint explicitly avoids database dependency, which prevents an unhealthy DB from turning into a process restart storm.

However, the current `ApplicationFactory` still registers only:

```text
/
/health
```

as routes.

It registers the three new Vanguard contracts, but does not register corresponding Router routes for:

```text
/health/live
/health/ready
/health/dependencies
```

So the controller methods exist, but the request routing currently does not expose them.

That's an implementation/composition mismatch.

There is another smaller concern:

```text
/health/dependencies
```

is documented as operator-only, but no authorization mechanism is attached to it yet.

---

# 11. ApplicationFactory is healthier now

The previous production-breaking bug has correctly been fixed:

```php
private readonly \Closure $log
```

instead of an untyped readonly property.

That was a genuine PHP 8.4 fatal and the fix is correct.

The bigger architectural issue from before still remains, though.

The request handler still does:

```php
catch (\Throwable $e)
```

and converts everything into a 500 response.

That means the earlier `PanicException` concern remains relevant.

If DGLab's doctrine says:

```text
PanicException
→ isolate
→ terminate worker
→ restart
```

then this generic catch is capable of intercepting the very exception that is supposed to escape the request boundary.

That should eventually become something conceptually like:

```php
catch (PanicException $e) {
    // log
    // terminate/recycle
    throw $e;
}

catch (Throwable $e) {
    // ordinary HTTP 500 handling
}
```

The same issue remains in the EventDispatcher.

So the architecture doctrine and runtime exception semantics are still not completely aligned.

---

# 12. The architecture linter is getting better, but it is still not a real dependency analyzer

The export allow-list has now matured to:

```text
Identity
Filesystem
Showcase
```

That's good.

But the implementation remains regex-based.

It scans:

```php
use Foo\Bar;
```

rather than actually analyzing PHP dependencies.

So it won't reliably see things expressed through:

```php
extends Foo
implements Bar
#[Foo]
new \Foo()
Foo::method()
return \Foo
```

This means:

> architecture boundary lint

still really means:

> imported namespace boundary lint.

That's useful, but the naming currently implies more coverage than it actually has.

There is also a portability problem that survived from the previous implementation:

```python
REPO_ROOT = Path("/home/z/my-project")
```

The GitHub runner does not normally check the repository out there.

So the canonical implementation should derive the root from the script:

```python
REPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
```

The checker also currently treats arbitrary unknown namespaces as:

```python
"external"
```

which means the external dependency boundary is effectively:

```text
known internal namespaces = restricted
everything else = allowed
```

rather than:

```text
explicitly approved external dependencies = allowed
unknown dependencies = rejected
```

That is an important difference for a project whose whole philosophy is executable architecture.

---

# 13. The export allow-list itself is a strong direction

The current public API model is becoming:

```text
Internal implementation
        │
        ├── not exported
        │
        ↓
Explicit package contract
```

For Identity:

```text
IdentityInterface
AuthenticatedUser
UserId
Email
RoleIdentifier
AuthMiddleware
```

For Filesystem:

```text
FilesystemInterface
Filesystem
FileMetadata
...
```

For Showcase:

```text
ProductApplicationInterface
CreateProductCommand
Product value objects
domain exceptions
```

This is a very good evolution.

The architectural boundary is no longer merely:

> "Don't import the wrong ring."

It becomes:

> "Even within an allowed ring, consumers can only depend on the package's declared public language."

That is much stronger.

---

# 14. The new SDLC/SPEC fusion is more important than it initially looks

`ARCHITECTURE-SDLC-FUSION.md` effectively establishes four authorities:

```text
SDLC
  → process, depth, throughput, cooldown

SPEC-001
  → architectural contracts

Generated architecture state
  → actual repository implementation

Fitness functions / CI
  → merge acceptance
```

That is a strong governance model.

It prevents an architecture document from silently becoming more authoritative than the code.

The latest ADR reconciliation also fixes several pieces of documentation drift, including:

```text
19 ADRs → 20
105 blueprints → 102
old MUWV claims → reconciled
PostgreSQL driver → correctly classified as future capability
```

So the project is now actively treating documentation drift as an engineering defect rather than cosmetic cleanup.

I think that is the correct direction.

---

# 15. But there is still a CI scalability problem

The existing package CI matrix wasn't modified in this eight-commit range.

The new packages are:

```text
core/filesystem
hub/identity
spoke/internal/showcase
```

yet the existing package CI architecture was previously a manually enumerated matrix.

That means every new package creates a question:

> Did somebody remember to add this package to CI?

That's already starting to hurt.

The architecture is now dynamic enough that a static package matrix is becoming inconsistent with the Composition Principle.

Eventually, CI should derive the package set from repository metadata, for example:

```text
packages/*/*/composer.json
        ↓
discover packages
        ↓
run package test/static-analysis jobs
```

Then adding:

```text
packages/spoke/internal/showcase
```

automatically adds the package to verification.

That would fit the new executable-architecture philosophy much better.

