# 16. Current architecture maturity

I'd describe the repository now as having four distinct maturity levels.

### Core

Very mature architecture.

```text
Container
Kernel
HTTP
Router
Middleware
Events
Crypto
DBAL
Filesystem
```

The boundaries are strong, although Filesystem needs hardening.

### Hub

Now becoming a genuine platform layer.

Identity is the first major example.

```text
Hub
├── Identity
├── Config
└── future shared capabilities
```

The most important remaining issue here is transactional persistence and standards-compliant JWT handling.

### Spokes

This is where the architecture gets interesting.

Showcase establishes:

```text
product-specific domain
↓
application service
↓
repository contract
↓
infrastructure adapter
```

This is the first concrete proof that the ring model can support an actual application rather than just framework infrastructure.

### Application

The composition root is now properly separated from Core.

`ApplicationFactory` is the right place for assembly.

But it is still mostly assembling:

```text
Hello
Health
Vanguard
Kernel
```

rather than composing actual products.

That is reasonable at current depth.

---

# 17. The architecture has crossed an important threshold

The previous DGLab architecture was essentially:

> Build an extremely strong runtime foundation and enforce its boundaries.

The new architecture is becoming:

> Build a strong runtime foundation, enforce its boundaries, then express real products entirely through those boundaries.

That's a much more meaningful test.

The first real composition is now:

```text
Showcase
   │
   ├── IdentityInterface
   ├── Core DBAL
   ├── Core EventDispatcher
   ├── Core HTTP
   └── Core Kernel / RequestContext
```

while the underlying implementation remains:

```text
Showcase
   ↓
Hub Identity
   ↓
Core
```

This is exactly the sort of dependency direction the original ring architecture was designed to achieve.

---

## My current architecture assessment

The repository has improved substantially since the previous snapshot.

The **largest positive changes** are:

1. **Products moved to Spokes**, separating product domains from shared Hub capabilities.
2. **Identity became a real bounded platform capability.**
3. **Showcase became the first real bounded product domain.**
4. **Public package surfaces are becoming explicit and enforceable.**
5. **Health semantics are now operationally separated.**
6. **Architecture governance is becoming part of the SDLC instead of just documentation.**

The **highest-priority technical issues I would address next** are:

```text
P0  Fix Filesystem root-containment check
P0  Make ES256 implementation RFC/JWS compatible
P0  Wire AuthMiddleware → authoritative RequestContext
P0  Protect /complete health route set and actually register the new routes

P1  Fix Showcase hydration of updatedAt/status
P1  Add authorization to publishProduct()
P1  Introduce explicit transaction boundary
P1  Fix stream write/read failure handling

P1  Fix architecture-lint repository-root portability
P1  Make external dependency policy explicit
P2  Move architecture lint from regex toward actual PHP AST analysis
P2  Auto-discover package CI instead of maintaining a manual matrix
P2  Resolve PanicException swallowing at request/event boundaries
```

The particularly interesting thing is that **DGLab is no longer primarily proving that its Core is well designed**. Showcase is now the first test of whether the entire architecture can carry an actual application without violating its own principles. That is a much harder and much more valuable test.

One final observation: the latest commit is documentation reconciliation rather than another implementation feature, which is consistent with the newly introduced **Cooldown 1** governance phase. The repository is beginning to treat architectural consolidation as a first-class engineering phase rather than continuously adding features.

