# 3. Identity's boundary became substantially stronger

PR #279 is arguably the most architecturally significant Lap 2 change.

It resolved three different classes of problem:

### Identity authority

`RequestContext.userId` is now explicitly authoritative, while the request attribute is treated as a transport convenience. The PR also adds a `stampRequestContext()` path for future container/pulse integration. ([GitHub](https://github.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab/pull/279))
That resolves the previous architectural ambiguity:

```text
Before

JWT
 ├──> RequestContext.userId
 └──> request attribute userId
       ↑
       potentially competing identity channels


After

JWT
  │
  ▼
RequestContext.userId   ← authoritative
  │
  └── request attribute ← transport convenience
```

This is a good example of **abstracting upward without duplicating state**.

### JWT interoperability

The implementation now converts OpenSSL's DER ECDSA signature to JWS `R || S` and back, and explicitly requires `alg === ES256`. The tests verify 64-byte signatures and reject wrong/missing algorithms and malformed/expired/invalid tokens. ([GitHub](https://github.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab/pull/279))
That changes Identity from "cryptographically plausible" to **protocol-interoperable**.

### Repository atomicity

`MySQLUserRepository::save()` is now transactional, making the user + roles update atomic. ([GitHub](https://github.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab/pull/279))
So Identity now has three important depth-3 properties:

**identity authority + protocol correctness + persistence atomicity.**

