I would fix it in the following sequence.

## 1. Freeze the definition of "drift"

Before changing anything, establish four classes:

| Class | Meaning | Authority |
|---|---|---|
| **Executable truth** | What the repository actually builds/runs | Composer, source tree, tests, CI |
| **Generated architecture state** | What tooling derives from executable truth | architecture-status generator |
| **Intentional architecture** | Decisions not inferable from code | ADRs / SPEC |
| **Narrative documentation** | Human explanation | README/docs |

The rule should become:

> **Narrative documentation may explain architecture, but may never be the authoritative source for repository structure.**

This is the most important correction.

---

# 2. Establish one canonical architecture-status command

You already have generated architecture status and several fitness functions. I would make them converge into one command:

```text
composer architecture:status
```

or, if you prefer the existing script convention:

```text
php scripts/architecture-status.php
```

It should emit something like:

```json
{
  "commit": "e74ad1d",
  "packages": {
    "total": 20,
    "core": 12,
    "hub": 2,
    "bridge": 1,
    "spoke_internal": 3,
    "spoke_external": 1
  },
  "dependencies": [...],
  "depth": {...},
  "fitness": {...},
  "generated_at": "...",
  "source": [
    "composer.json",
    "packages/**/composer.json",
    "Architecture/**",
    "tests/**"
  ]
}
```

The exact counts should of course be **derived**, not hard-coded.

The repository already has architecture tooling under `scripts/` and a generated-status fitness mechanism, so I would extend that rather than introduce another architecture subsystem. ([GitHub](https://github.com/DGCodeIdeas/DGLab))
